Faxbot: what the far end's fax machine said, Internet Aware Fax, and T.38 at once (spandsp 0.0.6).

The built-in engine (res_fax_spandsp) carries most of Faxbot's faxes but told
Faxbot nothing about the far end: not its capabilities (DIS), not the speeds
it trained at, not its internet address (CSA) or the subaddress it was given
(SUB). Three changes, the spandsp steps of each in faxbot_t38_gateway.h, which
the replay proof in asterisk/tests compiles as well:

1. Far-end frames. spandsp's real-time frame handler keeps, bounded to 32
   octets each, the far end's DIS (or DTC), the session's first and last DCS
   and which side sent it, each DCS's speed code, the CSA, TSA, SUB and NSF
   it received, and how many trainings and failures to train there were.
   When the session ends (phase E), they go on the channel as FAXBOT_FAR_DIS,
   FAXBOT_DCS_FIRST, FAXBOT_DCS_LAST, FAXBOT_DCS_SENT, FAXBOT_DCS_RATES,
   FAXBOT_FAR_CSA, FAXBOT_FAR_TSA, FAXBOT_FAR_SUB, FAXBOT_FAR_NSF,
   FAXBOT_TRAININGS, FAXBOT_FTT and FAXBOT_IAF_USED, for the dialplan's
   FaxFrames event, with one NOTICE line per session. res_pjsip_t38 adds when
   T.38 started and who asked for it (FAXBOT_T38_AT in epoch milliseconds,
   FAXBOT_T38_BY "far" or "faxbot"), once per channel.

2. Internet Aware Fax (IAF). On a call the dialplan marks FAXBOT_IAF ("peer":
   another Faxbot, both ends skip the training check; "endpoint": an IAF fax
   server an administrator approved), the T.38 terminal gets spandsp's IAF
   mode and sends faster than real time. spandsp's own unpaced mode is not
   used: in 0.0.6 it puts up to 300 octets in a T.38 packet and ignores the
   far end's datagram size, and Asterisk cuts any packet larger than the far
   end accepts (98 octets on Faxbot's trunks). Instead the T.38 front end's
   clock jumps to each next paced chunk, up to nine more per 20 ms tick, while
   the T.30 timers keep real time, and every queued packet goes out in that
   tick. Never set for a carrier call by guesswork.

3. T.38 at once. With FAXBOT_T38_NOW=yes, SendFAX asks for T.38 as soon as the
   call is answered instead of after three CNG tones (about ten seconds), for
   numbers whose calls never switched by themselves. A refusal goes on as
   audio on the same call, as before (SendFAX option f).

Nothing here changes a call Faxbot did not mark, beyond keeping the frames.

